%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: inferential analysis
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: 'The primary purpose of inferential statistics is to:'
    Options:
    - Describe the characteristics of a sample
    - Make predictions or generalizations about a population from a sample
    - Summarize data using mean and standard deviation
    - Organize and present data in tables and graphs
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A numerical characteristic of a population is called a:'
    Options:
    - Statistic
    - Parameter
    - Sample
    - Estimate
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The distribution of all possible values of a statistic from all
      possible samples of the same size is called a:'
    Options:
    - Population Distribution
    - Sample Distribution
    - Sampling Distribution
    - Normal Distribution
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The standard deviation of a sampling distribution is known as
      the:'
    Options:
    - Standard Error
    - Standard Deviation
    - Variance
    - Mean Deviation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The Central Limit Theorem states that the sampling distribution
      of the mean approaches a normal distribution as the sample size increases,
      regardless of the population''s shape, provided:'
    Options:
    - The population is normal
    - The sample size is small
    - The sample size is sufficiently large
    - The population mean is known
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A range of values within which a population parameter is estimated
      to lie is called a:'
    Options:
    - Point Estimate
    - Confidence Interval
    - Test Statistic
    - Significance Level
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis is known as:'
    Options:
    - "Type I Error (\u03B1)"
    - "Type II Error (\u03B2)"
    - Power
    - P-value
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The probability of failing to reject a false null hypothesis is
      known as:'
    Options:
    - "Type I Error (\u03B1)"
    - "Type II Error (\u03B2)"
    - Power
    - P-value
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The power of a statistical test is defined as:'
    Options:
    - The probability of a Type I error
    - The probability of a Type II error
    - "1 - \u03B1"
    - "1 - \u03B2"
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A p-value is the probability of obtaining a test statistic:'
    Options:
    - Exactly equal to the observed value
    - "As extreme as, or more extreme than, the observed value, assuming H\u2080
      is true"
    - "As extreme as, or more extreme than, the observed value, assuming H\u2080
      is false"
    - That proves the null hypothesis
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "If a result is statistically significant at the \u03B1 = 0.05
      level, it means:"
    Options:
    - The null hypothesis is false
    - The alternative hypothesis is true
    - The probability of the null hypothesis being true is less than 5%
    - The observed result would be unlikely (p < 0.05) if the null hypothesis were
      true
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A t-test is typically used instead of a z-test when:'
    Options:
    - The sample size is very large
    - The population standard deviation is known
    - The population standard deviation is unknown
    - The data is categorical
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The appropriate statistical test to compare the means of two independent
      groups is the:'
    Options:
    - Paired t-test
    - Independent samples t-test
    - One-sample t-test
    - ANOVA
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) is used to test:'
    Options:
    - Differences between two population means
    - Differences between two population variances
    - Differences among three or more population means
    - The relationship between two variables
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The non-parametric alternative to the independent samples t-test
      is the:'
    Options:
    - Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test
    - Kruskal-Wallis Test
    - Mann-Whitney U Test
    - Chi-square test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: If a 95% confidence interval for the difference between two means
      includes zero, what does this imply?
    Options:
    - There is a statistically significant difference between the means
    - There is no statistically significant difference between the means
    - The null hypothesis has been proven
    - The effect size is large
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The degree to which a sample statistic represents its corresponding
      population parameter is referred to as:'
    Options:
    - Reliability
    - Validity
    - Sampling Error
    - Generalizability
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The main reason for using a random sample is to:'
    Options:
    - Make the study easier to conduct
    - Minimize sampling bias and ensure the sample is representative
    - Guarantee that the sample is exactly like the population
    - Reduce the need for statistical tests
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "In hypothesis testing, the null hypothesis (H\u2080) typically
      represents:"
    Options:
    - The research hypothesis
    - The hypothesis of no effect or no difference
    - The hypothesis the researcher wants to prove
    - A statement of inequality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The F-ratio in a one-way ANOVA is a ratio of:'
    Options:
    - Between-groups variance to within-groups variance
    - Within-groups variance to between-groups variance
    - Total variance to error variance
    - Sample variance to population variance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: A researcher conducts a one-sample t-test and obtains a p-value
      of 0.02. This means that if the null hypothesis is true, the probability of
      obtaining a test statistic as extreme as the one observed is 2%. A student
      concludes, "There is a 2% probability that the null hypothesis is true." Why
      is this conclusion incorrect?
    Options:
    - "The p-value is calculated assuming H\u2080 is true; it is not the probability
      that H\u2080 is true."
    - The p-value should be greater than 0.05 to be significant.
    - The t-test was the wrong test to use.
    - The sample size was too small.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: A study with low statistical power is more susceptible to which
      type of error?
    Options:
    - Type I Error
    - Type II Error
    - Sampling Error
    - Measurement Error
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: A 99% confidence interval will be ______ a 95% confidence interval.
    Options:
    - Narrower than
    - Wider than
    - The same width as
    - Unrelated to
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'If you perform multiple statistical tests on the same data set
      without adjusting the significance level, you increase the overall risk of:'
    Options:
    - Type II Error
    - Type I Error
    - Reducing power
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The Pearson correlation coefficient (r) measures:'
    Options:
    - A cause-and-effect relationship
    - The strength and direction of a linear relationship
    - The difference between two means
    - The proportion of variance explained
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher finds a correlation of r = 0.15 between two variables
      with a p-value of 0.001. The best interpretation is:'
    Options:
    - The relationship is strong and significant.
    - The relationship is weak but statistically significant.
    - The relationship is strong but not significant.
    - The relationship is weak and not significant.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "The coefficient of determination (R\xB2) in regression analysis
      represents:"
    Options:
    - The slope of the regression line
    - The correlation between X and Y
    - The proportion of variance in Y explained by X
    - The standard error of the estimate
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'In a simple linear regression, a t-test is used to test if the
      slope is significantly different from zero. If the p-value for this test is
      high (p > 0.05), it suggests:'
    Options:
    - X is a significant predictor of Y
    - The regression model is useful
    - There is no significant linear relationship between X and Y
    - "The R\xB2 value is high"
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The non-parametric alternative to the one-way ANOVA is the:'
    Options:
    - Mann-Whitney U test
    - Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test
    - Kruskal-Wallis test
    - Friedman test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A Chi-square test of independence is used to determine if there
      is a significant association between:'
    Options:
    - Two means
    - Two medians
    - Two categorical variables
    - Two continuous variables
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "A researcher conducts a study with \u03B1=0.05 and power=0.80.
      What is the probability of a Type II error?"
    Options:
    - 0.05
    - 0.2
    - 0.8
    - 0.95
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'An effect size measure (like Cohen''s d) is considered crucial
      to report along with a p-value because it:'
    Options:
    - Determines statistical significance
    - Provides a measure of the magnitude of the effect, which is independent of
      sample size
    - Is required to calculate the p-value
    - Proves the null hypothesis is false
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: A 95% CI for a population mean is calculated from a sample as (45,
      55). Which of the following statements is correct?
    Options:
    - There is a 95% probability the population mean is between 45 and 55.
    - 95% of the sample data falls between 45 and 55.
    - 95% of such intervals, constructed from repeated sampling, would contain the
      population mean.
    - The population mean is definitely between 45 and 55.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'In a paired t-test, the null hypothesis is that the mean difference
      between the paired observations is:'
    Options:
    - Greater than zero
    - Less than zero
    - Equal to zero
    - Not equal to zero
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'If the assumptions of a parametric test (like normality) are violated,
      the consequence is that:'
    Options:
    - The p-value and confidence intervals may be inaccurate.
    - The test cannot be performed.
    - The effect size will be zero.
    - The null hypothesis is automatically true.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "A researcher wants to know if a new teaching method is more effective
      than the traditional one. The most appropriate alternative hypothesis (H\u2081)
      is:"
    Options:
    - "\u03BC_new = \u03BC_traditional"
    - "\u03BC_new \u2260 \u03BC_traditional"
    - "\u03BC_new < \u03BC_traditional"
    - "\u03BC_new > \u03BC_traditional"
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The standard error of the mean will decrease when:'
    Options:
    - The sample size decreases
    - The population standard deviation increases
    - The sample size increases
    - The confidence level increases
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "In the context of hypothesis testing, \"failing to reject H\u2080\"
      is not the same as \"accepting H\u2080\" because:"
    Options:
    - "It implies H\u2080 is probably true."
    - "It may simply mean there is insufficient evidence against H\u2080."
    - The p-value was greater than alpha.
    - The test was two-tailed.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A post-hoc test (like Tukey''s HSD) is conducted after a significant
      ANOVA result to:'
    Options:
    - Determine if the overall ANOVA model is significant
    - Identify which specific group means are significantly different from each other
    - Test the assumption of homogeneity of variances
    - Calculate the effect size
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "degrees of freedom" in a statistical test generally
      refers to:'
    Options:
    - The sample size
    - The number of values free to vary in a calculation
    - The level of significance
    - The power of the test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: A study finds a statistically significant result (p < 0.01) with
      a very large sample size (n=10,000), but the effect size (Cohen's d) is 0.02.
      What is the most accurate interpretation?
    Options:
    - The finding is highly significant and practically very important.
    - The finding is statistically significant but likely of little practical importance.
    - The null hypothesis should be accepted.
    - The p-value is invalid.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'In a regression analysis, an outlier can have a large influence
      on the results. A measure specifically designed to assess an observation''s
      influence on the entire regression model is:'
    Options:
    - The residual
    - The correlation coefficient
    - Cook's Distance
    - "The R\xB2 value"
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: "A researcher conducts 20 independent tests at \u03B1=0.05. If
      all null hypotheses are true, what is the approximate probability of making
      at least one Type I error?"
    Options:
    - 0.05
    - 0.36
    - 0.64
    - 0.95
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The assumption of "homoscedasticity" in linear regression means
      that:'
    Options:
    - The residuals are normally distributed
    - The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent
      variable
    - The relationship between X and Y is linear
    - There are no influential outliers
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A Bayesian statistician and a Frequentist statistician would most
      strongly disagree on the interpretation of:'
    Options:
    - The sample mean
    - A p-value
    - A confidence interval
    - The role of prior knowledge
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'If a test is "robust" to violations of its assumptions, it means
      that:'
    Options:
    - The assumptions are unnecessary
    - The test will always give the correct p-value
    - The p-value is relatively accurate even if assumptions are not perfectly met
    - The test can only be used with non-normal data
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher wants to compare the pre-test and post-test scores
      of the same group of students. The most appropriate test is a:'
    Options:
    - Independent samples t-test
    - Paired samples t-test
    - One-way ANOVA
    - Chi-square test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The main purpose of using a Bonferroni correction is to:'
    Options:
    - Increase the power of a test
    - Control the family-wise Type I error rate
    - Control the Type II error rate
    - Simplify the calculations
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'In a meta-analysis, a "forest plot" is primarily used to:'
    Options:
    - Show the distribution of individual study data
    - Visually display the effect sizes and confidence intervals of multiple studies
    - Test for publication bias
    - Check for heterogeneity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
  - QuestionText: 'The null hypothesis for a Chi-square goodness-of-fit test is that:'
    Options:
    - The two variables are independent
    - The sample data fits the claimed distribution
    - The population means are equal
    - The variances are homogeneous
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: inferential analysis
